# Problem Statement

Students juggle assignments, timetables, deadlines, and study time across
subjects, but usually track them scattered across notebooks, phone notes,
or memory. This makes it easy to forget deadlines, lose track of how much
work is actually done, and study subjects unevenly. There is no single,
simple tool a first-year student can use to see everything in one place
without needing a complicated app or a paid service.

# Scope of the Project

This project is a command-line Python application that allows a single
student to:

- Add, view, complete, and delete academic tasks
- Assign a priority (High / Medium / Low) and a deadline to each task
- Maintain a simple weekly timetable
- Log study time per subject
- View overall task progress as a percentage
- View a one-screen dashboard summarizing tasks, progress, and study time

**Out of scope for this version:**
- Saving data permanently between runs (no file or database storage)
- Multiple user accounts or login
- A graphical user interface (GUI)
- Notifications, reminders, or calendar integration
- Searching or filtering tasks

# Target Users

- First-year (and other) college students who want a simple, no-setup way
  to track academic tasks, a weekly timetable, and study time
- Anyone learning basic Python who wants to see these concepts (lists,
  loops, functions, conditionals) applied in a small real project

# High-Level Features

1. Task Manager — add, view, complete, and delete tasks with subject,
   priority, and deadline
2. Timetable — add a class to a day and view the weekly timetable
3. Study Time Tracker — log and view hours studied per subject
4. Progress Report — calculates completed vs. pending tasks as a percentage
5. Dashboard — a single summary screen showing tasks, progress, and study
   time at a glance
